        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01EX8X65Cr7SwmociCNPfYTA",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/ingest-governance.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Ingest governance\\ndescription: Rules and processes that control how external sources are admitted into a managed knowledge system.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Knowledge-Management\\n  - concept/Governance\\n  - pattern/Data-Quality\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single authoritative source; describes a systematic practice in knowledge management systems.\\u0022\\nreviewed: false\\n---\\n\\nIngest governance is the set of rules and processes that control how external sources are admitted into a managed knowledge system. It typically defines:\\n\\n- **Authorization**: who or what (a human editor or an autonomous agent) is authorized to write to the system\\n- **Source trust levels**: which sources are trusted versus treated as untrusted data\\n- **Conflict resolution**: how conflicting or duplicate information is handled (update, supersede, or flag a contradiction)\\n- **Audit trail**: what records are kept for every ingest decision, so the reasoning behind a page\\u0027s current state can be reconstructed later\\n\\n## Rationale\\n\\nIngest governance is essential in knowledge systems where multiple sources may contribute to the same topics. It provides:\\n\\n- **Traceability**: Understanding why a piece of information is in the system and what decision was made about its precedence\\n- **Quality control**: Ensuring that trusted sources take precedence over untrusted ones, and that contradictions are handled consistently\\n- **Reproducibility**: Being able to replay the reasoning that led to the current state of any page or topic\\n- **Accountability**: Clear records of who authorized which changes and when\\n\\n## Implementation considerations\\n\\nIngest governance frameworks typically address:\\n\\n- Role-based or capability-based authorization models\\n- Confidence scoring or source credibility assessment\\n- Conflict detection and resolution policies\\n- Retention policies for superseded or contradicted information\\n- Audit logging and traceability features\\n\u0022}"
        }
